  <h2 id="sales">Who pays for these skills, and how they judge you</h2>

  <p>
    Labs, firms and researchers pay for reliable analysis or reliable lab work. They judge you by accuracy, records and reproducibility.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first basic skill is making a method note someone else could repeat.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and bioinformatics versus biotechnology: which tasks are under pressure</h2>

  <p>
    AI tools write code and analyse data. Routine scripts shrink first, which affects bioinformatics beginners.
  </p>
  <p>
    Biological judgment, study design and accountability hold value. A staged path helps: now, use AI to speed coding; later, own the design and interpretation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="better">Alternatives that can beat a general bioinformatics versus biotechnology path</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Alternative</th>
          <th>Why it can beat it</th>
          <th>Catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Data science route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Wider employers</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Less biology</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Computer science route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Strong coding base</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Needs biology</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Pharma route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Regulated products</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Strict rules</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="learn">How to learn bioinformatics versus biotechnology cheaply, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    Bioinformatics versus biotechnology is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free NPTEL and SWAYAM biology and statistics lessons.</li>
    <li>Free public datasets and open-source tools.</li>
    <li>Free YouTube lab-technique videos for theory.</li>
    <li>A lab visit or a teacher who reviews your record.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    In two weeks, run both trials and write which one you would repeat.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

